The attached image, Figure 11 from the *Journal of Controlled Release* article, illustrates the local inflammatory response in skeletal muscle two weeks after intramuscular injection of vaccine adjuvants. The left panel (AH) shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (indicated by asterisks) with numerous aggregates of macrophages, characteristic of the long-lasting response induced by aluminum hydroxide adjuvant. In contrast, the right panel (Nano-11) demonstrates significantly less residual inflammation, with relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords in the connective tissue, indicating a more transient and resolved inflammatory response caused by the Nano-11 adjuvant.

The image can significantly contribute to cancer research by informing the development of safer and more effective cancer immunotherapies. Adjuvants are crucial for therapeutic cancer vaccines, and Nano-11's demonstrated ability to activate dendritic cells and enhance immune responses with a transient, mild local inflammatory profile—as depicted by the reduced inflammation compared to aluminum hydroxide—suggests it could improve patient tolerability and compliance for repeated vaccinations, a key factor in sustained anti-tumor immunity.